<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lambert of Hersfeld</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1028 – c. 1082</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lambert of Hersfeld was a Benedictine monk of Hersfeld Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and one of the leading German chroniclers of the 11th century. His Annals, a world chronicle that becomes detailed from 1040, give a vivid and hostile account of King Henry IV's reign, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> revolt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> up to 1077. He later became abbot of Hasungen.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>What little is known of Lambert's life comes from scattered details in his own writings. Also called Lampert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>, he was probably a Franconian of good family. He prepared for an ecclesiastical career at the cathedral school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, where he was taught by Anno of Steusslingen, later Archbishop Anno II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. He entered Hersfeld as a monk on 15 March 1058, was ordained priest at Aschaffenburg on 16 September and then made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back at Hersfeld by October 1059, Lambert worked in the cloister library and taught in the monastery school. In 1071 he visited the abbeys of Siegburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> to study the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluniac-reforms">Cluniac Reforms</a> promoted by Anno, but he held to the traditional Benedictine rule and stayed reserved towards monastic reform. In 1077, at the instigation of Henry IV's enemy Archbishop Siegfried I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, he moved to the canon monastery of Hasungen and made it a Benedictine abbey settled with monks from Hirsau. Circumstantial evidence suggests he served as its first abbot from 1081, and he probably died shortly afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Annals open with a universal history from the creation of the world, drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a> and German annals such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> and Weissenburg; from the early 1040s the account is Lambert's own. It ends with the election of Rudolf of Rheinfelden as anti-king in 1077, which Lambert called a fitting conclusion. He wrote a classicising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> full of allusions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terence">Terence</a>. He was probably also the author of a life of Saint Lullus, founder of Hersfeld, and a polemic history of the abbey that survives only in later excerpts. Kaspar Currer first printed the Annals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> in 1525.</p><p>Lambert favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> and the regional aristocracy, and his judgements, above all of the Walk to Canossa, dominated the German picture of Henry IV until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a> of the nineteenth century. Historians trained in source criticism then called him a partisan writer, and his editor Oswald Holder-Egger called him an outright fabulist in places. Modern scholars read him as a voice of the conservative regional aristocracy and elite monasticism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lambert of Hersfeld” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its scholars included the chronicler <b>Lambert of Hersfeld</b>, who wrote a major account of the reign of Henry IV.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hersfeld">Principality of Hersfeld</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Hasungen Abbey, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.82,10.87&amp;year=1063" aria-label="Franconia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lambert of Hersfeld"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5596 5817h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5596 5817h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.82,10.87&amp;year=1063">What was here in 1063? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
